    (v_topic_id, 'A 96-year-old female named Evelyn has developed severe, irreversible septic shock and multi-organ failure. She is unresponsive. Her designated healthcare proxy, her brother, is asked whether to initiate hemodialysis. The proxy states: ''We never discussed kidney failure or dialysis, but I know how much she loved her independence and always said she would never want to live in a vegetative state or dependent on machines. Based on that, I know she would say no to dialysis.'' Which ethical decision-making standard is the proxy applying?', '["Distributive justice; where treatment is withheld based on the financial cost to the hospital and society", "Therapeutic privilege; where the surrogate defers all decision-making to the medical team''s personal values", "Best interest standard; where the surrogate chooses the option that a reasonable person would select to maximize objective clinical benefit", "Substituted judgment; where the surrogate makes the decision they believe the patient would have made for themselves based on the patient''s known values, goals, and prior statements", "Autologous proxy standard; where the physician makes the decision and the surrogate simply signs the consent form"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **substituted judgment standard** is the primary ethical standard used by surrogate decision-makers when a patient lacks capacity but has left some indication of their values or preferences. Under this standard, the surrogate does not choose what they personally want, nor what they think is best for the patient; instead, they **step into the patient''s shoes** and make the decision that the **patient would make for themselves** based on their known personality, lifestyle, values, religious beliefs, and previous statements. (Ref: UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Clara has developed severe, irreversible septic shock and multi-organ failure. She is unresponsive. Her designated healthcare proxy, her brother, is asked whether to initiate hemodialysis. The proxy states: ''We never discussed kidney failure or dialysis, but I know how much she loved her independence and always said she would never want to live in a vegetative state or dependent on machines. Based on that, I know she would say no to dialysis.'' Which ethical decision-making standard is the proxy applying?', '["Distributive justice; where treatment is withheld based on the financial cost to the hospital and society", "Therapeutic privilege; where the surrogate defers all decision-making to the medical team''s personal values", "Autologous proxy standard; where the physician makes the decision and the surrogate simply signs the consent form", "Substituted judgment; where the surrogate makes the decision they believe the patient would have made for themselves based on the patient''s known values, goals, and prior statements", "Best interest standard; where the surrogate chooses the option that a reasonable person would select to maximize objective clinical benefit"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The **substituted judgment standard** is the primary ethical standard used by surrogate decision-makers when a patient lacks capacity but has left some indication of their values or preferences. Under this standard, the surrogate does not choose what they personally want, nor what they think is best for the patient; instead, they **step into the patient''s shoes** and make the decision that the **patient would make for themselves** based on their known personality, lifestyle, values, religious beliefs, and previous statements. (Ref: UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named William is admitted with a perforated sigmoid colon and fecal peritonitis. He is severely demented, non-verbal, and has no designated healthcare proxy, no advance directives, and no living relatives or friends. A decision must be made regarding emergency laparostomy. Since the patient''s previous values and wishes are completely unknown, which ethical decision-making standard must guide the surrogate or temporary guardian?', '["Paternalistic beneficence standard; where the medical team is legally required to perform all possible surgeries regardless of prognosis", "Best interest standard; where the decision-maker selects the option that maximizes clinical benefit and minimizes harm, choosing what a reasonable person would want in this situation", "Substituted judgment standard; where the decision-maker guesses what the patient would have wanted based on general statistical demographics", "Subliminal surrogate standard; where the decision is deferred until a relative is located via ancestry databases", "Physician autonomy standard; where the surgeon proceeds with their own personal preference without any oversight"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When an incapacitated patient''s **preferences, values, and previous wishes are completely unknown** (e.g., a patient with no family, friends, or advance directives), the surrogate or court-appointed guardian must use the **best interest standard**. Under this standard, the decision-maker must select the option that **maximizes the patient''s well-being and minimizes pain and suffering**, effectively choosing what a **reasonable person** would want in that specific clinical scenario, weighing the benefits against the burdens of the intervention. (Ref: UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
