atient to another facility. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male named Raymond is admitted to a skilled nursing facility for rehabilitation following a hip fracture. During the admission process, the administrative staff provides Raymond with written information regarding his right to formulate advance directives. Which of the following federal statutes mandates this requirement?', '["The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA); which governs the privacy of advance directives", "The Patient Self-Determination Act (PSDA) of 1990; which requires Medicare- and Medicaid-funded healthcare institutions to inform patients of their rights under state law to accept or refuse treatment and to formulate advance directives", "The Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA); which makes advance directives mandatory for all disabled individuals", "The Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act (EMTALA); which requires emergency stabilization regardless of advance directives", "The Medicare Access and CHIP Reauthorization Act (MACRA); which ties physician reimbursement to the number of living wills signed"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Patient Self-Determination Act (PSDA) of 1990** is a federal law that applies to all healthcare institutions (hospitals, nursing homes, hospice programs, HMOs) receiving Medicare or Medicaid funding. The PSDA requires these facilities to: (1) **Provide written information to patients** upon admission regarding their rights under state law to make decisions about their medical care, including the right to accept or refuse treatment and the right to formulate advance directives. (2) **Document** in the medical record whether the patient has an advance directive. (3) **Educate staff and the community** about advance directives. (Ref: PSDA 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
