    (v_topic_id, 'A 80-year-old female named Ruth is admitted to a skilled nursing facility for rehabilitation following a hip fracture. During the admission process, the administrative staff provides Ruth with written information regarding her right to formulate advance directives. Which of the following federal statutes mandates this requirement?', '["The Emergency Medical Treatment and Active Labor Act (EMTALA); which requires emergency stabilization regardless of advance directives", "The Patient Self-Determination Act (PSDA) of 1990; which requires Medicare- and Medicaid-funded healthcare institutions to inform patients of their rights under state law to accept or refuse treatment and to formulate advance directives", "The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA); which governs the privacy of advance directives", "The Medicare Access and CHIP Reauthorization Act (MACRA); which ties physician reimbursement to the number of living wills signed", "The Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA); which makes advance directives mandatory for all disabled individuals"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The **Patient Self-Determination Act (PSDA) of 1990** is a federal law that applies to all healthcare institutions (hospitals, nursing homes, hospice programs, HMOs) receiving Medicare or Medicaid funding. The PSDA requires these facilities to: (1) **Provide written information to patients** upon admission regarding their rights under state law to make decisions about their medical care, including the right to accept or refuse treatment and the right to formulate advance directives. (2) **Document** in the medical record whether the patient has an advance directive. (3) **Educate staff and the community** about advance directives. (Ref: PSDA 1990; Hazzard''s Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old female named Mildred has stage G5 chronic kidney disease and has been undergoing hemodialysis for 3 years. She presents to her nephrologist and states that she is exhausted by the therapy, experiences severe fatigue, and wishes to discontinue hemodialysis permanently, understanding that doing so will lead to death within 2 weeks. Psychiatry confirms she has no active depression or delirium, and she demonstrates full capacity. What is the most appropriate clinical action?', '["Involuntary admit the patient to a psychiatric facility for suicidal ideation, and continue dialysis against her will", "Inform the patient that because they are choosing to stop dialysis, they are ineligible for any medical care or pain management", "Prescribe high-dose oral antidepressants and continue hemodialysis until the medications achieve therapeutic levels in 6 weeks", "Obtain a court order to force the patient to undergo dialysis, as suicide is illegal and hemodialysis is life-sustaining", "Respect the patient''s autonomous decision to refuse life-sustaining treatment; discontinue hemodialysis, and initiate a comprehensive palliative care plan to manage uremic symptoms (e.g., pruritus, dyspnea, nausea) and ensure a comfortable death"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A **competent patient has the absolute ethical and legal right to refuse any medical treatment**, including life-sustaining interventions (such as mechanical ventilation, feeding tubes, or hemodialysis). The physician''s duties are to: (1) Confirm the patient''s **decision-making capacity**, (2) Ensure the patient is **fully informed** of the consequences (death), (3) Address any treatable issues (e.g., pain, depression) that might be influencing the decision, and (4) **Respect the refusal, transition to comfort-focused care, and manage symptoms** (in uremia: dyspnea, nausea, pruritus, agitation) to ensure a peaceful death. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
