    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Edith has end-stage dementia and is being considered for hospice care. Which of the following correctly outlines the eligibility criteria for a patient to enroll in the Medicare Hospice Benefit?', '["The patient must agree to sign a waiver stating they will never visit an emergency department or hospital for any reason", "The patient must have a terminal prognosis of less than 30 days, certified by a court-appointed judge, and must undergo a trial of chemotherapy first", "The patient must be completely bedbound and requiring mechanical ventilation to qualify for any hospice services", "The patient must have a terminal illness with a prognosis of 6 months or less if the disease runs its normal course, certified by two physicians (the primary care physician and the hospice medical director), and the patient must agree to waive curative treatment for the terminal condition", "Hospice eligibility is determined solely by the patient''s age; all individuals aged >= 85 are automatically enrolled"]'::jsonb, 3, 'To qualify for the **Medicare Hospice Benefit**, the patient must meet three core criteria: (1) **Terminal prognosis of 6 months or less** if the terminal illness runs its normal course. (2) **Certification** of the terminal illness by **two physicians** (typically the patient''s attending physician and the hospice medical director; subsequent recertifications require only the hospice physician). (3) **Election of the benefit**: The patient (or surrogate if they lack capacity) must sign a statement choosing hospice care and **waiving Medicare coverage for curative treatments** of the terminal condition. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
