    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Florence is diagnosed with Stage IV lung adenocarcinoma. She wants to receive specialized medical care to manage her severe pain and dyspnea, but she also wishes to undergo a new palliative chemotherapy regimen that may slow tumor progression. Which of the following is correct regarding the distinction between palliative care and hospice care for this patient?', '["There is no difference; the two terms are identical and both require the patient to waive all medications including pain control", "Palliative care can be initiated at any stage of a serious illness and can be provided concurrently with curative/disease-modifying treatments (like chemotherapy), whereas hospice care is a subset of palliative care for patients with a terminal prognosis of <= 6 months who waive curative therapy", "Palliative care is only provided in long-term nursing homes, while hospice care is exclusively provided in acute care hospitals", "Hospice care is funded by private insurance only, whereas palliative care is a mandatory benefit under Medicare Part A", "Hospice care allows the patient to undergo aggressive curative surgeries, whereas palliative care is strictly limited to patients in their last 48 hours of life"]'::jsonb, 1, '**Palliative care** is specialized medical care focused on providing relief from the symptoms and stress of a serious illness, with the goal of improving quality of life. It **can be initiated at any stage of a serious illness** (regardless of prognosis) and can be **provided concurrently with curative, life-prolonging, or disease-modifying therapies** (e.g., chemotherapy, radiation, dialysis). **Hospice care** is a specific delivery model of palliative care for patients with a **prognosis of <= 6 months** who are no longer pursuing curative or disease-modifying treatments. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 82; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
