    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Joseph has advanced Parkinson''s disease and is enrolled in home hospice care. His primary caregiver, his spouse, is experiencing severe physical exhaustion and caregiver burnout. The hospice nurse suggests utilizing the ''Respite Care'' benefit. Which of the following is correct regarding the Medicare hospice respite care benefit?', '["Respite care is a cash benefit paid directly to the caregiver to purchase vacation packages", "The respite benefit can only be used once in a patient''s lifetime and is limited to a maximum of 12 hours", "Respite care allows the caregiver to hire a full-time private nurse to live in their home permanently at no cost", "Respite care requires the patient to be admitted to the hospital ICU to undergo diagnostic testing for caregiver stress", "The patient can be admitted to an approved inpatient facility (e.g., nursing home or hospice unit) for up to 5 consecutive days to provide temporary relief to the primary caregiver, funded by the Medicare hospice benefit"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Under the Medicare Hospice Benefit, **Respite Care** is one of the four levels of hospice care. It is designed to provide temporary relief to the patient''s primary caregiver (usually a family member). Respite care allows the patient to be transferred to an Medicare-approved inpatient facility (such as a nursing home, assisted living, or inpatient hospice unit) for **up to 5 consecutive days per episode**. There is no limit on the number of times it can be used, but it must be scheduled in advance and is not intended for acute symptom management. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old female named Esther has advanced Parkinson''s disease and is enrolled in home hospice care. Her primary caregiver, her spouse, is experiencing severe physical exhaustion and caregiver burnout. The hospice nurse suggests utilizing the ''Respite Care'' benefit. Which of the following is correct regarding the Medicare hospice respite care benefit?', '["The patient can be admitted to an approved inpatient facility (e.g., nursing home or hospice unit) for up to 5 consecutive days to provide temporary relief to the primary caregiver, funded by the Medicare hospice benefit", "Respite care allows the caregiver to hire a full-time private nurse to live in their home permanently at no cost", "Respite care requires the patient to be admitted to the hospital ICU to undergo diagnostic testing for caregiver stress", "Respite care is a cash benefit paid directly to the caregiver to purchase vacation packages", "The respite benefit can only be used once in a patient''s lifetime and is limited to a maximum of 12 hours"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under the Medicare Hospice Benefit, **Respite Care** is one of the four levels of hospice care. It is designed to provide temporary relief to the patient''s primary caregiver (usually a family member). Respite care allows the patient to be transferred to an Medicare-approved inpatient facility (such as a nursing home, assisted living, or inpatient hospice unit) for **up to 5 consecutive days per episode**. There is no limit on the number of times it can be used, but it must be scheduled in advance and is not intended for acute symptom management. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Edwin has advanced Parkinson''s disease and is enrolled in home hospice care. His primary caregiver, his spouse, is experiencing severe physical exhaustion and caregiver burnout. The hospice nurse suggests utilizing the ''Respite Care'' benefit. Which of the following is correct regarding the Medicare hospice respite care benefit?', '["Respite care allows the caregiver to hire a full-time private nurse to live in their home permanently at no cost", "Respite care is a cash benefit paid directly to the caregiver to purchase vacation packages", "The respite benefit can only be used once in a patient''s lifetime and is limited to a maximum of 12 hours", "Respite care requires the patient to be admitted to the hospital ICU to undergo diagnostic testing for caregiver stress", "The patient can be admitted to an approved inpatient facility (e.g., nursing home or hospice unit) for up to 5 consecutive days to provide temporary relief to the primary caregiver, funded by the Medicare hospice benefit"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Under the Medicare Hospice Benefit, **Respite Care** is one of the four levels of hospice care. It is designed to provide temporary relief to the patient''s primary caregiver (usually a family member). Respite care allows the patient to be transferred to an Medicare-approved inpatient facility (such as a nursing home, assisted living, or inpatient hospice unit) for **up to 5 consecutive days per episode**. There is no limit on the number of times it can be used, but it must be scheduled in advance and is not intended for acute symptom management. (Ref: AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; Hazzard''s Ch. 82)'),
